    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Mountains and Highlands: Alborz, Zagros and Beyond | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore how Iran’s mountains shape rainfall, drainage and landscapes, from the Alborz barrier to folded Zagros ridges and volcanic summits."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "reading-the-highlands",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "More than a collection of summits",
        "content": "Iran’s mountain geography is easiest to understand through contrasts between slopes, valleys, and adjoining lowlands. In the north, the Alborz separates the Caspian depression from the elevated interior. Its northern and southern sides differ sharply in climate and landscape. In the Zagros, satellite-derived relief reveals repeated ridges and intervening valleys, expressing the folding of layered rocks. These are distinct mountain settings, rather than interchangeable examples of high ground. [s1][s3]\n\nA useful guide therefore asks several questions alongside the name of a peak: which mountain system contains it, what rocks and structures shape it, which direction its slopes face, and where its water drains. This approach connects the individual summit pages in this branch with the wider geography of Iran. The contrast between a volcanic cone such as Damavand and folded sedimentary terrain in the Zagros is particularly instructive. [s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "alborz",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The Alborz: a barrier with contrasting sides",
        "content": "The Alborz forms the central section of Iran’s northern mountain arc. Its relief separates the Caspian lowlands from the central uplands, creating an important ecological boundary. The Sefidrud valley marks a major break toward its western end, while the range connects eastward with the mountains of Khorasan. Within it, high massifs, deep valleys, and passes make a more intricate landscape than the outline of a single continuous ridge suggests. [s1]\n\nThe range also contains different kinds of high mountain scenery. The Takht-e Solayman massif includes Alam Kuh, while Damavand rises as a volcanic cone in the central Alborz. Their shared regional setting does not make their landforms geologically equivalent. A range-wide overview establishes their position; the individual mountain pages examine what distinguishes each place. [s1][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "zagros",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The Zagros: folding made visible",
        "content": "In the Zagros, the approach of Arabia toward Asia compresses the crust and folds sedimentary layers. NASA’s Shuttle Radar Topography Mission imagery shows upward-arching folds, called anticlines, and intervening downward folds, called synclines. Erosion exposes these structures unevenly because different rock layers resist removal differently. The resulting patterns explain why a relief map can appear as a sequence of elongated ridges rather than isolated cones. [s3]\n\nSalt adds another distinctive process in parts of southern Iran. Buried salt can rise through overlying rocks and emerge at the surface. Where it spreads downslope, it forms features commonly called salt glaciers. The name describes their flowing shape; the moving material is salt. These formations show why the geology beneath a mountain landscape matters as much as its visible outline. Folded rocks, erosion, and mobile salt can operate together, producing terrain that a simple elevation ranking would not explain. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "climate-and-water",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why exposure matters",
        "content": "Mountain barriers redistribute moisture. Air approaching exposed slopes can produce precipitation, while sheltered terrain farther inland is drier. The northern Alborz flanks and western Zagros slopes are important examples of relatively well-watered mountain margins. Moving across a ridge can therefore mean crossing an ecological boundary as well as gaining or losing altitude. [s2]\n\nSeasonality matters too. Snow stores part of the cold-season precipitation in mountain areas and releases water during melting. The historical climatic synthesis in Encyclopaedia Iranica connects this seasonal release with streams, groundwater, and cultivation. It provides a useful explanation of the physical relationship, rather than a measurement of present-day snow reserves. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mountain-water-diagram",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "A simplified mountain moisture pathway",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Moist air approaches mountains\"] -->|\"Rises along exposed slopes\"| B[\"Precipitation on mountain margins\"]\n  B -->|\"Cold conditions permit storage\"| C[\"Seasonal snow cover\"]\n  C -->|\"Melting releases water\"| D[\"Streams and groundwater recharge\"]\n  A -->|\"Mountains limit inland moisture penetration\"| E[\"Drier sheltered interior\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows describe a general climatic and hydrological relationship. They do not quantify rainfall, snow storage, or the contribution of any particular mountain to a river.",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "exploring-the-branch",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From regional systems to individual places",
        "content": "This branch brings together Damavand, Alam Kuh, Sabalan, Zard Kuh, and Dena, alongside a comparison of the Alborz and Zagros ranges. It is best explored at two scales: mountain systems explain broad spatial relationships, while individual pages examine specific landforms and evidence. The same distinction should guide interpretation of maps and photographs. A snow-covered summit, a folded ridge, and an exposed salt flow may all belong to Iran’s highlands, but they reveal different processes. [s1][s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
