    "seo": {
      "title": "Damavand: Iran’s Highest Mountain and Its Volcanic History | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Damavand’s volcanic landforms, dated eruption evidence, thermal activity, differing elevation figures and UNESCO Tentative List record."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mountain-and-volcano",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Iran’s highest mountain",
        "content": "Damavand rises within the Alborz mountain range northeast of Tehran. It is Iran’s highest mountain and a stratovolcano: a volcanic edifice built through successive eruptions. Its setting combines the regional geography of a mountain chain with the distinct form of an individual volcano. NASA’s astronaut-photography account shows how snow can emphasize solidified lava flows on the western flank, making parts of that eruptive history visible from space. [s1]\n\nPublished elevations require care. The Smithsonian Global Volcanism Program lists 5,670 metres, whereas Iran’s UNESCO Tentative List submission gives approximately 5,628 metres. NASA’s page itself contains both figures in different passages. These sources establish Damavand’s exceptional height but do not resolve the measurement discrepancy. The figures should be attributed to their sources rather than presented as evidence of a change in the mountain’s elevation. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "eruptive-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A cone built over a longer history",
        "content": "The Global Volcanism Program describes a younger cone constructed over an older volcanic edifice. Activity was concentrated mainly at the summit, sending lava flows outward across the slopes. Its geological summary places construction of the younger cone within roughly the past 600,000 years, a much longer history than the recent lava surfaces alone might suggest. [s2]\n\nThe database records a confirmed eruptive period at approximately 5350 BCE, with an uncertainty of 200 years, based on uranium-series isotopic evidence. This is a geological age estimate, not a dated eyewitness account. No historically recorded eruptions are identified in the profile. Keeping those categories separate avoids turning an approximate prehistoric date into an artificially precise historical event. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "thermal-features",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Quiet does not mean thermally inactive",
        "content": "Hot springs occur on Damavand’s flanks, and fumaroles—vents that release volcanic gases and steam—have been documented near the summit crater. NASA interprets these features as evidence for a hot or cooling body of magma beneath the volcano and describes Damavand as dormant rather than extinct. This is a geological explanation, not a forecast of an eruption. [s1]\n\nThe Smithsonian profile includes summit observations from 1993 and 1999, including sulfur deposits and fumarolic activity. Those reports document conditions during particular visits. They should not be repurposed as a current gas-emission survey or evidence of newly developing unrest. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "heritage-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A heritage proposal with defined limits",
        "content": "Iran submitted Damavand to the UNESCO World Heritage Tentative List on 5 February 2008. The submission emphasizes thermal springs, plant communities, and endemic flora. A Tentative List entry records a state’s proposed heritage property; it is not itself World Heritage inscription. UNESCO also makes clear that responsibility for the entry’s content rests with the submitting state. [s3]\n\nThis distinction matters when describing the surrounding environment. The submission provides evidence of the values Iran advanced for recognition, while detailed claims about present biodiversity or conservation effectiveness require additional ecological research. Damavand can therefore be understood through complementary records: volcanic research explains its construction and thermal features, imagery reveals landforms, and the heritage submission documents an institutional proposal. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
